{"step_index":845,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T11:23:10Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T11:23:10Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T11:23:11Z\nShowing lines 1-50 of 234\n1: ---\n2: name: grpc-expert\n3: description: \"Expert guidance for gRPC/Protocol Buffers work in the operation-dbus-proto (OP-DBUS) workspace — designing plugin methods, generating .proto/service definitions, wiring the op-grpc-bridge, or fixing plugins with missing gRPC methods. Use this whenever the user mentions gRPC, proto/protobuf, plugin methods, the gRPC bridge, tonic, reflection descriptors, 'missing plugins' in the gRPC diagnostic page, or the seal/freeze/hot pipeline — even if they don't say 'gRPC' explicitly (e.g. 'the plugin isn't showing up on the diagnostic page', 'add a method to plugin X', 'regen protos').\"\n4: metadata:\n5:   version: 1.0.0\n6: disable-model-invocation: false\n7: ---\n8: \n9: # gRPC Expert — OP-DBUS\n10: \n11: This workspace does **not** hand-write per-plugin `.proto` files. Proto generation is\n12: automatic, driven entirely by each plugin's `PluginSchema`. If you remember one thing\n13: from this skill: **fixing a plugin's gRPC surface means editing its Rust schema\n14: function, not writing `.proto`.**\n15: \n16: ## The real pipeline (roll → seal → freeze → hot)\n17: \n18: ```\n19: 1. Plugin declares  → PluginSchema { methods: HashMap<String, MethodDecl>, signals: Vec<SignalDecl>, ... }\n20:                        in crates/op-plugins/src/state_plugins/<plugin>.rs\n21: 2. Schema gets put   → op-projection's SchemaEngine publishes the catalog hash\n22:                        (op_identity::schema_bridge::schema_catalog_hash())\n23: 3. Sealed by blob    → op-blob seals the plugin object into an immutable blob:\n24:                        /dev/shm/opdbus/plugin-blobs/<plugin_id>.<schema_hash16>.blob\n25:                        (op-blob is the ONLY writer — never write blobs elsewhere)\n26: 4. Descriptors       → TWO separate reflection layers exist, don't confuse them:\n27:    for reflection       a) STATIC: crates/op-grpc-bridge/build.rs generates\n28:                             plugin_methods.proto from every plugin's schema.methods,\n29:                             compiles it with tonic_build into operation_descriptor.bin\n30:                             (a protobuf FileDescriptorSet), served by tonic-reflection.\n31:                          b) DYNAMIC: crates/op-grpc-bridge/src/dynamic_reflection.rs\n32:                             (ActiveReflectionCatalog) hydrates live from the sealed\n33:                             SHM blob catalog via hydrate_reflection_from_shm() —\n34:                             this is the \"frozen\" descriptor set for what's actually\n35:                             present right now, independent of what's compiled in.\n36: 5. Frozen            → the blob + its descriptors are immutable once sealed.\n37: 6. gRPC socket = hot → crates/op-grpc-bridge/src/grpc_server.rs registers the\n38:                        generated per-plugin service (plugin_method_routes.rs,\n39:                        add_routes()) on the tonic UDS bridge, dispatching each\n40:                        RPC to call_generated_plugin_method_typed(plugin_id,\n41:                        schema_name, ...) which routes through PluginService.CallMethod\n42:                        — i.e. gRPC never bypasses D-Bus, it's a bridge, not a\n43:                        second control plane.\n44: ```\n45: \n46: A plugin shows green/connected on the gRPC diagnostic page **iff**:\n47: - its `PluginSchema.methods` is non-empty (empty → `build.rs` silently skips it —\n48:   see `collect_plugin_methods()`), **and**\n49: - the plugin is sealed in the SHM blob catalog.\n50: "}
{"step_index":846,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T11:23:10Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T11:23:10Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T11:23:11Z\nShowing lines 1-50 of 63\n1: ---\n2: name: grpc-protocol-expert\n3: description: >-\n4:   Generic specialist in gRPC protocol, streaming RPCs, Protocol Buffers, channel\n5:   config, load balancing, auth, and observability. Use for general gRPC/protobuf\n6:   work outside the OP-DBUS PluginSchema seal/freeze/hot pipeline. For OP-DBUS\n7:   plugin gRPC surfaces, prefer the grpc-expert skill instead.\n8: disable-model-invocation: false\n9: ---\n10: \n11: # gRPC Protocol Expert\n12: \n13: ## Focus Areas\n14: \n15: - gRPC protocol intricacies and best practices\n16: - Unary, server-streaming, client-streaming, and bidirectional streaming RPCs\n17: - Protocol Buffers (protobuf) for efficient serialization\n18: - Service definition and implementation in gRPC\n19: - Channel configuration and management\n20: - Load balancing strategies within gRPC\n21: - gRPC authentication and authorization mechanisms\n22: - Network optimization for gRPC communication\n23: - Observability setups, including logging, tracing, and metrics\n24: - Efficient handling of gRPC errors and status codes\n25: \n26: ## Approach\n27: \n28: - Begin with a clear understanding of service requirements before implementing\n29: - Use Protocol Buffers for defining service interfaces and messages\n30: - Implement efficient error handling with gRPC status codes\n31: - Leverage streaming for real-time data processing where applicable\n32: - Optimize network usage by compressing messages and headers\n33: - Employ deadline and timeouts for better control over communication\n34: - Choose appropriate load balancing strategies for scalability\n35: - Configure multiple channels and target services for robustness\n36: - Utilize SSL/TLS for secure communication\n37: - Implement structured logging, tracing, and metrics setup for observability\n38: \n39: ## Quality Checklist\n40: \n41: - Thoroughly defined .proto files adhering to defined conventions\n42: - Service implementation matches the .proto specification\n43: - Correctly configured server and client channels\n44: - Stream types appropriately used based on data flow needs\n45: - Efficient serialization and deserialization processes\n46: - Comprehensive unit and integration testing for gRPC calls\n47: - Implemented error handling with descriptive status codes\n48: - Adequate logging of gRPC requests and responses\n49: - Metrics capturing for latency, error rates, and payload size\n50: - Secure communication ensured with proper encryption standards"}
{"step_index":847,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T11:23:11Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T11:23:11Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T11:23:11Z\nShowing lines 1-50 of 377\n1: ---\n2: name: json-render\n3: description: >-\n4:   Expert skill for building json-render.dev catalog components, page specs, and\n5:   generative UI across three repos: /srv/git/json-render (library source),\n6:   /srv/git/odbus (Rust backend + op-web Axum server), and\n7:   /srv/git/3tched.com (separate chatbot-rendered UI repository).\n8:   Covers the full stack: defineCatalog → Zod schema → React component →\n9:   pageSpec → NAV_MANIFEST → factory plugin wiring → sealed blob pipeline →\n10:   gallery/catalog promotion gate. Also covers the D-Bus plugin schema for\n11:   json-render and schema-renderer in op-plugins.\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # json-render Skill\n15: \n16: ## Local deployment policy — runit, not systemd\n17: \n18: The user-designated UI repository is `/srv/git/3tched.com`, separate\n19: from `/srv/git/odbus`. Inspect its actual catalog, registry and build/sync inputs;\n20: the reference layouts below do not establish its implemented components.\n21: `/srv/git/operation-dashboard-ui-07` and `crates/op-web/ui/` are retired UI paths.\n22: \n23: The host uses runit. Backend runtime actions go through the authenticated D-Bus\n24: plugin surface. Only for authorized operator service work use `sudo -n sv`\n25: (for example `sudo -n sv status op-web`); never use `systemctl`, its shim, s6 or\n26: service6. Edit versioned runit inputs, not `/run/runit/service`. Backend deployment\n27: is btrfs send/receive of an explicitly prepared filesystem snapshot; there is no\n28: deployment script. Never hand-copy binaries. Static UI synchronization does not itself\n29: require an op-web restart. Do not create a parallel frontend backend or expose\n30: broker credentials to the browser.\n31: \n32: EMQX is the retained vendor-binary-backed `emqx` D-Bus plugin, independent of\n33: retired Netmaker. Broker events may drive authorized live state updates while\n34: the chatbot generates schema-pinned layout patches. A generated mode, component\n35: or action never grants permissions: enforce verified session and project ACLs\n36: server-side, including on streamed updates and reconnects.\n37: \n38: ## Overview — Three Repos, One System\n39: \n40: ```\n41: /srv/git/json-render                   ← upstream library (source of truth for API)\n42: /srv/git/odbus                         ← Rust backend\n43:   crates/op-plugins/src/state_plugins/json_render.rs    D-Bus plugin schema\n44:   crates/op-plugins/src/state_plugins/schema_renderer.rs\n45:   crates/op-web/src/handlers/ui_model.rs                Axum gallery/catalog routes\n46: /srv/git/3tched.com        ← active UI target; paths below are layout examples\n47:   src/json-render/\n48:     catalog/catalog.ts                 defineCatalog (app catalog contract)\n49:     catalog/registry.tsx               defineRegistry (component implementations)\n50:     catalog/components/                React component render functions"}
{"step_index":848,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T11:23:11Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T11:23:11Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T11:23:11Z\nShowing lines 1-50 of 309\n1: ---\n2: name: ovs-db-analysis\n3: description: >-\n4:   Analyze Open vSwitch data from sosreport — conf.db, OpenFlow dumps, port stats,\n5:   tunnels, and datapath health. Use when troubleshooting OVS packet drops, flow\n6:   performance, bridge/port/interface issues, DPDK vs kernel datapath, or when the\n7:   user mentions sosreport openvswitch, ovs-ofctl, ovsdb-tool, or ovs-db-analysis.\n8: ---\n9: \n10: # OVS Database and Flow Analysis\n11: \n12: This skill provides detailed analysis of Open vSwitch (OVS) data collected via sosreport. It operates in four modes:\n13: \n14: 1. **Default mode**: Full analysis - conf.db + all text files (requires ovsdb-tool, falls back to text files if not available)\n15: 2. **Database mode (`--db`)**: Database only - analyze conf.db (requires ovsdb-tool)\n16: 3. **Text files mode (`--flows-only`)**: Text files only - no ovsdb-tool needed\n17: 4. **Query mode (`--query`)**: Run custom OVSDB JSON queries (requires ovsdb-tool)\n18: \n19: ## When to Use This Skill\n20: \n21: Use this skill when:\n22: - **Troubleshooting packet drops** - Find drop flows being hit\n23: - **Analyzing flow performance** - View top flows by packet count\n24: - Analyzing OVS configuration from a sosreport\n25: - Troubleshooting bridge, port, or interface issues\n26: - Investigating DPDK vs kernelspace datapath\n27: - Reviewing tunnel configurations (VXLAN, Geneve, GRE)\n28: - Checking port statistics (drops, errors, traffic)\n29: - Mapping Kubernetes pods to OVS interfaces\n30: \n31: ## Prerequisites\n32: \n33: **Default mode** (full analysis):\n34: - **ovsdb-tool** must be installed (from openvswitch package)\n35: - Falls back to `--flows-only` if ovsdb-tool not found\n36: - Sosreport with `sos_commands/openvswitch/` directory and `conf.db`\n37: \n38: **Database mode** (`--db`):\n39: - **ovsdb-tool** must be installed (from openvswitch package)\n40:   - Check: `which ovsdb-tool`\n41:   - Fedora/RHEL: `sudo dnf install openvswitch`\n42:   - Ubuntu/Debian: `sudo apt install openvswitch-common`\n43: - Sosreport with `conf.db` file\n44: \n45: **Text files mode** (`--flows-only`):\n46: - No special tools needed - works out of the box!\n47: - Sosreport with `sos_commands/openvswitch/` directory\n48: \n49: **Query mode** (`--query`):\n50: - **ovsdb-tool** must be installed"}
{"step_index":849,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T11:23:11Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T11:23:11Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T11:23:12Z\nShowing lines 1-50 of 423\n1: ---\n2: name: kernel-fuse-diagnosis\n3: description: >\n4:   FUSE（用户态文件系统）内核端故障诊断技能。当用户提到 FUSE 文件系统 hang、\n5:   所有操作返回 EIO、FUSE daemon 崩溃/重启、请求队列阻塞、D 状态进程堆积、\n6:   /sys/fs/fuse/connections/ 异常、libfuse 兼容性、max_read/max_write 配置不当、\n7:   writeback cache 一致性问题、多线程 FUSE daemon 死锁、容器中 /dev/fuse 权限问题、\n8:   FUSE 性能退化等关键词时，必须使用本技能。\n9:   覆盖场景：FUSE daemon 崩溃后 EIO、请求队列阻塞与 D 状态、max_read/max_write 配置不当、\n10:   writeback cache 一致性问题、多线程 daemon 死锁、/dev/fuse 权限问题、内核 FUSE 模块 Bug、\n11:   混合复杂 FUSE 故障。即使只收到\"文件系统挂了\"、\"目录打不开\"、\"ls 卡死\"但挂载点\n12:   为 FUSE 类型时也应触发本技能。\n13:   支持 FUSE 全链路诊断（sysfs 队列 → 内核参数 → daemon 进程 → 代码级根因）。\n14: ---\n15: \n16: # FUSE 内核端故障诊断（三层下钻：系统层 → 类型层 → 代码根因层）\n17: \n18: ## 第一节：故障目录结构\n19: \n20: ```text\n21: fuse_case/                  # 故障案例目录\n22: ├── scripts/                # 【内置】本技能的诊断脚本\n23: │   ├── 01_baseline_info.sh\n24: │   ├── branch_A_daemon_crash.sh\n25: │   ├── branch_B_req_queue.sh\n26: │   ├── branch_C_max_read_write.sh\n27: │   ├── branch_D_writeback_cache.sh\n28: │   ├── branch_E_mt_deadlock.sh\n29: │   ├── branch_F_dev_fuse_perm.sh\n30: │   ├── branch_G_kernel_bug.sh\n31: │   └── branch_H_mixed.sh\n32: ├── logs/                   # 【可选】应用日志\n33: │   ├── application.log\n34: │   └── dmesg_output.txt\n35: ├── fuse_state/             # 【可选】故障时刻的 FUSE 状态快照\n36: │   ├── connections.txt     # ls /sys/fs/fuse/connections/\n37: │   ├── waiting.txt         # cat .../waiting\n38: │   └── abort.txt           # cat .../abort\n39: └── report/                 # 【输出】诊断报告目录\n40: ```\n41: \n42: ```bash\n43: # 检查 FUSE 文件系统挂载状态基线\n44: mount -t fuse\n45: ```\n46: \n47: ---\n48: \n49: ## 第二节：分析策略（三层下钻，逐层收敛）\n50: "}
