{"step_index":863,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T11:23:42Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T11:23:42Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T11:23:44Z\nShowing lines 1-50 of 302\n1: ---\n2: title: Audit Rubric\n3: description: Multi-dimension audit rubric with pass/warn/fail criteria, weighted scoring methodology, automated vs manual checklists, and audit report format\n4: tags:\n5:   [\n6:     audit-rubric,\n7:     pass-warn-fail,\n8:     weighted-scoring,\n9:     automated-checks,\n10:     manual-review,\n11:     scoring-methodology,\n12:   ]\n13: ---\n14: \n15: # Audit Rubric\n16: \n17: A structured rubric for executing quality audits with clear pass/warn/fail thresholds, weighted scoring, and a distinction between automated and manual audit items.\n18: \n19: ## Pass / Warn / Fail Thresholds\n20: \n21: Each dimension receives a 1-10 score, then maps to a threshold:\n22: \n23: | Threshold | Score Range | Meaning                                         | Action Required           |\n24: | --------- | ----------- | ----------------------------------------------- | ------------------------- |\n25: | Pass      | 7-10        | Meets or exceeds standards                      | No action                 |\n26: | Warn      | 5-6         | Below standard but functional                   | Improvement plan required |\n27: | Fail      | 1-4         | Critical gaps, blocks release or further work   | Immediate remediation     |\n28: | N/A       | --          | Dimension does not apply (e.g., no UI for a11y) | Redistribute weight       |\n29: \n30: **Hard fail conditions (override any score):**\n31: \n32: - Phase 0 resource completeness fails -- cap overall at 6.0\n33: - Any security dimension score of 3 or below -- flag as release blocker\n34: - Any critical anti-pattern found without a mitigation plan -- fail the audit\n35: \n36: ## Dimension Rubric Summary\n37: \n38: Each dimension uses consistent criteria across three levels. See `dimension-rubrics.md` for full per-dimension detail.\n39: \n40: ### Code Quality (10%)\n41: \n42: | Level | Criteria                                                      |\n43: | ----- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |\n44: | Pass  | Consistent patterns, low duplication, complexity under limits |\n45: | Warn  | Some code smells, moderate duplication, complexity hotspots   |\n46: | Fail  | No patterns, widespread duplication, god objects              |\n47: \n48: ### Architecture (10%)\n49: \n50: | Level | Criteria                                                        |"}
{"step_index":864,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T11:23:43Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T11:23:43Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T11:23:44Z\nShowing lines 1-50 of 185\n1: ---\n2: title: Anti-Patterns Guide\n3: description: Catalog of code, architecture, security, testing, and process anti-patterns to identify during quality audits with detection strategies\n4: tags:\n5:   [\n6:     anti-patterns,\n7:     code-smells,\n8:     security-vulnerabilities,\n9:     testing-antipatterns,\n10:     process-issues,\n11:   ]\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # Anti-Patterns Guide\n15: \n16: A catalog of anti-patterns to identify during quality audits, organized by category. Each anti-pattern includes detection strategies and severity levels.\n17: \n18: ## Code Anti-Patterns\n19: \n20: | Anti-Pattern           | Signal                                   | Severity |\n21: | ---------------------- | ---------------------------------------- | -------- |\n22: | God objects            | Single class/module doing everything     | High     |\n23: | Spaghetti code         | No clear flow, tangled dependencies      | High     |\n24: | Copy-paste programming | Duplicated blocks with minor variations  | Medium   |\n25: | Magic numbers          | Unexplained literals in logic            | Medium   |\n26: | Global state abuse     | Mutable shared state without controls    | High     |\n27: | Deep nesting           | 4+ levels of indentation                 | Medium   |\n28: | Long methods           | Functions exceeding 50 lines             | Medium   |\n29: | Primitive obsession    | Using primitives instead of value types  | Low      |\n30: | Feature envy           | Method uses more of another class's data | Medium   |\n31: | Dead code              | Unreachable or unused code paths         | Low      |\n32: | Type bypassing         | `any` and `@ts-ignore` causing drift     | High     |\n33: | Debug artifacts        | `console.log` and test scaffolding left  | Medium   |\n34: \n35: ### Detection Strategies\n36: \n37: ```bash\n38: # Find functions over 50 lines (approximate)\n39: rg -c \"^}\" -g \"*.ts\" | sort -t: -k2 -rn | head -20\n40: \n41: # Find potential magic numbers\n42: rg \"\\b(?:100|200|404|500|1000|3600|86400)\\b\" -g \"*.ts\" --stats\n43: \n44: # Find duplicated string literals\n45: rg -o '\"[^\"]{10,}\"' -g \"*.ts\" --no-filename | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -10\n46: \n47: # Find deeply nested code\n48: rg \"^\\s{16,}\" -g \"*.ts\" -c | sort -t: -k2 -rn\n49: \n50: # Find type bypassing"}
{"step_index":865,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T11:23:43Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T11:23:43Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T11:23:44Z\nShowing lines 1-50 of 120\n1: ---\n2: name: verification-before-completion\n3: description: Use when about to claim work is complete, fixed, or passing, before committing or creating PRs - requires running verification commands and confirming output before making any success claims; evidence before assertions always\n4: ---\n5: \n6: # Verification Before Completion\n7: \n8: ## Overview\n9: \n10: **Core principle:** Evidence before claims, always.\n11: \n12: **Violating the letter of this rule is violating the spirit of this rule.**\n13: \n14: ## The Iron Law\n15: \n16: ```\n17: NO COMPLETION CLAIMS WITHOUT FRESH VERIFICATION EVIDENCE\n18: ```\n19: \n20: If you haven't run the verification command in this message, you cannot claim it passes.\n21: \n22: ## The Gate Function\n23: \n24: ```\n25: BEFORE claiming any status or expressing satisfaction:\n26: \n27: 1. IDENTIFY: What command proves this claim?\n28: 2. RUN: Execute the FULL command (fresh, complete)\n29: 3. READ: Full output, check exit code, count failures\n30: 4. VERIFY: Does output confirm the claim?\n31:    - If NO: State actual status with evidence\n32:    - If YES: State claim WITH evidence\n33: 5. ONLY THEN: Make the claim\n34: \n35: Skip any step = lying, not verifying\n36: ```\n37: \n38: ## Common Failures\n39: \n40: | Claim | Requires | Not Sufficient |\n41: |-------|----------|----------------|\n42: | Tests pass | Test command output: 0 failures | Previous run, \"should pass\" |\n43: | Linter clean | Linter output: 0 errors | Partial check, extrapolation |\n44: | Build succeeds | Build command: exit 0 | Linter passing, logs look good |\n45: | Bug fixed | Test original symptom: passes | Code changed, assumed fixed |\n46: | Regression test works | Red-green cycle verified | Test passes once |\n47: | Agent completed | VCS diff shows changes | Agent reports \"success\" |\n48: | Requirements met | Line-by-line checklist | Tests passing |\n49: \n50: ## Red Flags - STOP"}
{"step_index":866,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T11:23:43Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T11:23:43Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T11:23:44Z\nShowing lines 1-50 of 182\n1: ---\n2: name: agent-self-evaluation\n3: description: Use after completing any non-trivial task. The agent self-rates its output on 5 axes — accuracy, completeness, clarity, actionability, conciseness — with concrete evidence per criterion. Produces a structured 1-5 scorecard with specific improvement suggestions.\n4: origin: ECC\n5: ---\n6: \n7: # Agent Self-Evaluation\n8: \n9: After completing a complex task, the agent pauses to rate its own output against a structured 5-axis rubric. This is NOT a pass/fail gate — it's a deliberate reflection step that catches omissions, flags overconfidence, and surface areas for improvement before the user has to.\n10: \n11: ## When to Activate\n12: \n13: - After writing code that spans 3+ files or 50+ lines\n14: - After completing a multi-step workflow (implement → test → review)\n15: - After a debugging session that involved 3+ attempts\n16: - After producing a design document, architecture decision, or written analysis\n17: - When the user asks \"how good was that?\" or \"rate yourself\"\n18: - At the end of any session Stop hook (if configured — see `references/hook-integration.md`)\n19: \n20: ## Core Concepts\n21: \n22: ### The 5 Evaluation Axes\n23: \n24: | Axis | Question | What it catches |\n25: |---|---|---|\n26: | **Accuracy** | Are the facts, claims, and outputs correct? | Hallucinations, wrong API names, incorrect syntax, false statements |\n27: | **Completeness** | Did it cover everything the user asked for? | Missed edge cases, unhandled error paths, forgotten requirements, skipped subtasks |\n28: | **Clarity** | Is the explanation understandable and well-structured? | Confusing explanations, jargon without definition, missing context, rambling |\n29: | **Actionability** | Can the user act on the output immediately? | Vague suggestions, missing steps, \"you should X\" without showing how, no verification path |\n30: | **Conciseness** | Did it use the minimum words/tokens needed? | Redundancy, over-explanation, repeating the user's question verbatim, filler content |\n31: \n32: ### Scoring Scale\n33: \n34: ```\n35: 5 — Exceptional: no reasonable improvement possible\n36: 4 — Good: minor nits only, no substantive gaps\n37: 3 — Adequate: meets the request but has a notable weakness on at least one axis\n38: 2 — Weak: has a clear gap that affects usability or correctness\n39: 1 — Poor: fundamentally misses the request or contains significant errors\n40: ```\n41: \n42: ### The Evidence Rule\n43: \n44: Every score below 5 MUST cite specific evidence. A score of 3 cannot just say \"could be better\" — it must say exactly what is missing or wrong. The mantra: **\"Show the gap, don't just name it.\"**\n45: \n46: ## Workflow\n47: \n48: ### Step 1: Collect the Raw Material\n49: \n50: Gather what you'll evaluate:"}
{"step_index":867,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T11:23:43Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T11:23:43Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T11:23:44Z\nShowing lines 1-50 of 126\n1: ---\n2: name: application-security\n3: description: 'Application security covering threat modeling (STRIDE), OWASP Top 10 (2025), OWASP API Security Top 10 (2023), secure coding review, authentication/authorization patterns, input validation, encryption, security headers, supply chain security, compliance (GDPR/HIPAA/SOC2/PCI-DSS), and security monitoring. Use when reviewing code for vulnerabilities, implementing auth patterns, securing APIs, configuring security headers, hardening supply chain, preventing injection attacks, or preparing for compliance audits.'\n4: license: MIT\n5: metadata:\n6:   author: oakoss\n7:   version: '1.1'\n8: user-invocable: false\n9: ---\n10: \n11: # Security\n12: \n13: Security is built-in, not bolted-on. Every feature, endpoint, and data flow must consider security implications.\n14: \n15: ## OWASP Top 10 (2025)\n16: \n17: | #   | Vulnerability                      | Prevention                                      |\n18: | --- | ---------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |\n19: | 1   | Broken Access Control              | Verify permissions server-side, default deny    |\n20: | 2   | Security Misconfiguration          | Secure defaults, remove unused features         |\n21: | 3   | Software Supply Chain Failures     | SBOM, dependency scanning, signed builds        |\n22: | 4   | Cryptographic Failures             | Use TLS, hash passwords (argon2id), encrypt PII |\n23: | 5   | Injection                          | Parameterized queries, input validation         |\n24: | 6   | Insecure Design                    | Threat modeling, security requirements          |\n25: | 7   | Authentication Failures            | Strong passwords, MFA, secure session mgmt      |\n26: | 8   | Software or Data Integrity         | Verify dependencies, sign releases              |\n27: | 9   | Logging and Alerting Failures      | Log security events, set up alerts              |\n28: | 10  | Mishandling Exceptional Conditions | Fail securely, generic errors to clients        |\n29: \n30: ## Security Principles\n31: \n32: | Principle          | Rule                                                      |\n33: | ------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------- |\n34: | Defense in Depth   | Multiple layers: firewall, auth, authz, encryption, audit |\n35: | Least Privilege    | Minimum permissions needed, nothing more                  |\n36: | Zero Trust         | Never trust, always verify. Assume breach.                |\n37: | Secure by Default  | HTTPS, strict passwords, secure cookies out of the box    |\n38: | Fail Securely      | Access denied on error, no internal details to users      |\n39: | Validate on Server | Client validation is UX, server validation is security    |\n40: \n41: ## Pre-Deployment Checklist\n42: \n43: | Area          | Requirements                                                        |\n44: | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n45: | Passwords     | Hashed with argon2id (preferred) or bcrypt (12+ rounds)             |\n46: | Tokens        | JWT with EdDSA/ES256, 15min access / 7d refresh, httpOnly cookies   |\n47: | Sessions      | HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite=Strict cookies                           |\n48: | Rate Limiting | Auth endpoints: 5 attempts/15min                                    |\n49: | Authorization | All routes check auth server-side, default deny                     |\n50: | Input         | Validated with schema (Zod), parameterized SQL                      |"}
{"step_index":868,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T11:23:44Z","input_tokens":4276,"cache_read_tokens":44841,"output_tokens":377,"tool_calls":[{"name":"read_file","args":{"EndLine":"50","Path":"\"/srv/git/odbus-verified/.agents/skills/rust/SKILL.md\"","StartLine":"1"}},{"name":"read_file","args":{"EndLine":"50","Path":"\"/srv/git/odbus-verified/.agents/skills/rust-patterns/SKILL.md\"","StartLine":"1"}},{"name":"read_file","args":{"EndLine":"50","Path":"\"/srv/git/odbus-verified/.agents/skills/rust-testing/SKILL.md\"","StartLine":"1"}},{"name":"read_file","args":{"EndLine":"50","Path":"\"/srv/git/odbus-verified/.agents/skills/svelte/SKILL.md\"","StartLine":"1"}},{"name":"read_file","args":{"EndLine":"50","Path":"\"/srv/git/odbus-verified/.agents/skills/svelte-code-writer/SKILL.md\"","StartLine":"1"}}]}
{"step_index":869,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T11:23:51Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T11:23:51Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T11:23:52Z\nShowing lines 1-50 of 81\n1: ---\n2: name: rust\n3: description: |\n4:   Rust language patterns and ecosystem covering ownership, borrowing, lifetimes, error handling (Result/Option, thiserror, anyhow), async with Tokio, traits, generics, serde serialization, Cargo workspaces, and testing. Includes axum web framework, ratatui terminal UI, clap CLI parsing, criterion benchmarking, cargo-deny supply chain security, cargo-dist binary distribution, and release-plz changelog automation.\n5: \n6:   Use when writing Rust code, designing ownership models, building async services with Tokio/axum, creating terminal UIs with ratatui, setting up CLI applications with clap, configuring CI/CD pipelines for Rust, benchmarking with criterion, auditing dependencies with cargo-deny, or distributing binaries with cargo-dist.\n7: license: MIT\n8: metadata:\n9:   author: oakoss\n10:   version: '1.0'\n11:   source: 'https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/'\n12: user-invocable: false\n13: ---\n14: \n15: # Rust\n16: \n17: ## Overview\n18: \n19: Rust is a systems programming language focused on safety, concurrency, and performance. The ownership system guarantees memory safety without garbage collection, and the type system enforces thread safety at compile time.\n20: \n21: **When to use:** Systems programming, web services (axum), CLI tools (clap), terminal UIs (ratatui), WebAssembly, performance-critical applications, anything requiring memory safety without runtime overhead.\n22: \n23: **When NOT to use:** Rapid prototyping where compile times matter more than safety, simple scripting tasks, projects where the team has no Rust experience and deadlines are tight.\n24: \n25: ## Quick Reference\n26: \n27: | Pattern             | API / Tool                          | Key Points                           |\n28: | ------------------- | ----------------------------------- | ------------------------------------ |\n29: | Ownership transfer  | `let b = a;`                        | `a` is moved, no longer usable       |\n30: | Borrowing           | `&T` / `&mut T`                     | One mutable OR many immutable refs   |\n31: | Lifetime annotation | `fn f<'a>(x: &'a str) -> &'a str`   | Ties output lifetime to input        |\n32: | Error propagation   | `?` operator                        | Converts and propagates errors       |\n33: | Custom errors       | `thiserror::Error` derive           | Structured error types with Display  |\n34: | Ad-hoc errors       | `anyhow::Result<T>`                 | Context chaining for applications    |\n35: | Async runtime       | `#[tokio::main]`                    | Entry point for async programs       |\n36: | Spawn task          | `tokio::spawn(async { })`           | Concurrent async task execution      |\n37: | HTTP router         | `axum::Router::new().route(...)`    | Composable routing with extractors   |\n38: | Extractors          | `Json<T>`, `Path<T>`, `State<T>`    | Type-safe request parsing            |\n39: | Serialization       | `#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]` | serde with format-agnostic derives   |\n40: | CLI parsing         | `#[derive(Parser)]`                 | clap derive API for arg parsing      |\n41: | TUI rendering       | `terminal.draw(\\|f\\| { })`          | Immediate-mode ratatui rendering     |\n42: | Benchmarking        | `criterion::Criterion`              | Statistical benchmarking framework   |\n43: | Dep auditing        | `cargo deny check`                  | License, vulnerability, source audit |\n44: | Binary release      | `cargo dist init`                   | Cross-platform binary distribution   |\n45: | Changelog           | `release-plz update`                | Auto semver bump and changelog       |\n46: \n47: ## Common Mistakes\n48: \n49: | Mistake                                 | Correct Pattern                               |\n50: | --------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |"}
{"step_index":870,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T11:23:51Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T11:23:51Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T11:23:52Z\nShowing lines 1-50 of 500\n1: ---\n2: name: rust-patterns\n3: description: Idiomatic Rust patterns, ownership, error handling, traits, concurrency, and best practices for building safe, performant applications. Use when writing or reviewing Rust code and ownership, error handling, traits, or concurrency is in question.\n4: metadata:\n5:   origin: ECC\n6: ---\n7: \n8: # Rust Development Patterns\n9: \n10: Idiomatic Rust patterns and best practices for building safe, performant, and maintainable applications.\n11: \n12: ## When to Use\n13: \n14: - Writing new Rust code\n15: - Reviewing Rust code\n16: - Refactoring existing Rust code\n17: - Designing crate structure and module layout\n18: \n19: ## How It Works\n20: \n21: This skill enforces idiomatic Rust conventions across six key areas: ownership and borrowing to prevent data races at compile time, `Result`/`?` error propagation with `thiserror` for libraries and `anyhow` for applications, enums and exhaustive pattern matching to make illegal states unrepresentable, traits and generics for zero-cost abstraction, safe concurrency via `Arc<Mutex<T>>`, channels, and async/await, and minimal `pub` surfaces organized by domain.\n22: \n23: ## Core Principles\n24: \n25: ### 1. Ownership and Borrowing\n26: \n27: Rust's ownership system prevents data races and memory bugs at compile time.\n28: \n29: ```rust\n30: // Good: Pass references when you don't need ownership\n31: fn process(data: &[u8]) -> usize {\n32:     data.len()\n33: }\n34: \n35: // Good: Take ownership only when you need to store or consume\n36: fn store(data: Vec<u8>) -> Record {\n37:     Record { payload: data }\n38: }\n39: \n40: // Bad: Cloning unnecessarily to avoid borrow checker\n41: fn process_bad(data: &Vec<u8>) -> usize {\n42:     let cloned = data.clone(); // Wasteful — just borrow\n43:     cloned.len()\n44: }\n45: ```\n46: \n47: ### Use `Cow` for Flexible Ownership\n48: \n49: ```rust\n50: use std::borrow::Cow;"}
{"step_index":871,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T11:23:51Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T11:23:51Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T11:23:52Z\nShowing lines 1-50 of 501\n1: ---\n2: name: rust-testing\n3: description: Rust testing patterns including unit tests, integration tests, async testing, property-based testing, mocking, and coverage. Follows TDD methodology. Use when writing Rust tests — unit, integration, async, property-based, or coverage.\n4: metadata:\n5:   origin: ECC\n6: ---\n7: \n8: # Rust Testing Patterns\n9: \n10: Comprehensive Rust testing patterns for writing reliable, maintainable tests following TDD methodology.\n11: \n12: ## When to Use\n13: \n14: - Writing new Rust functions, methods, or traits\n15: - Adding test coverage to existing code\n16: - Creating benchmarks for performance-critical code\n17: - Implementing property-based tests for input validation\n18: - Following TDD workflow in Rust projects\n19: \n20: ## How It Works\n21: \n22: 1. **Identify target code** — Find the function, trait, or module to test\n23: 2. **Write a test** — Use `#[test]` in a `#[cfg(test)]` module, rstest for parameterized tests, or proptest for property-based tests\n24: 3. **Mock dependencies** — Use mockall to isolate the unit under test\n25: 4. **Run tests (RED)** — Verify the test fails with the expected error\n26: 5. **Implement (GREEN)** — Write minimal code to pass\n27: 6. **Refactor** — Improve while keeping tests green\n28: 7. **Check coverage** — Use cargo-llvm-cov, target 80%+\n29: \n30: ## TDD Workflow for Rust\n31: \n32: ### The RED-GREEN-REFACTOR Cycle\n33: \n34: ```\n35: RED     → Write a failing test first\n36: GREEN   → Write minimal code to pass the test\n37: REFACTOR → Improve code while keeping tests green\n38: REPEAT  → Continue with next requirement\n39: ```\n40: \n41: ### Step-by-Step TDD in Rust\n42: \n43: ```rust\n44: // RED: Write test first, use todo!() as placeholder\n45: pub fn add(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 { todo!() }\n46: \n47: #[cfg(test)]\n48: mod tests {\n49:     use super::*;\n50:     #[test]"}
{"step_index":872,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T11:23:52Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T11:23:52Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T11:23:52Z\nShowing lines 1-50 of 286\n1: ---\n2: name: svelte\n3: description: Svelte 5 renderer for json-render that turns JSON specs into Svelte component trees. Use when working with @json-render/svelte, building Svelte UIs from JSON, creating component catalogs, or rendering AI-generated specs.\n4: ---\n5: \n6: # @json-render/svelte\n7: \n8: Svelte 5 renderer that converts json-render specs into Svelte component trees.\n9: \n10: ## Quick Start\n11: \n12: ```svelte\n13: <script lang=\"ts\">\n14:   import { Renderer, JsonUIProvider } from \"@json-render/svelte\";\n15:   import type { Spec } from \"@json-render/svelte\";\n16:   import Card from \"./components/Card.svelte\";\n17:   import Button from \"./components/Button.svelte\";\n18: \n19:   interface Props {\n20:     spec: Spec | null;\n21:   }\n22: \n23:   let { spec }: Props = $props();\n24:   const registry = { Card, Button };\n25: </script>\n26: \n27: <JsonUIProvider>\n28:   <Renderer {spec} {registry} />\n29: </JsonUIProvider>\n30: ```\n31: \n32: ## Creating a Catalog\n33: \n34: ```typescript\n35: import { defineCatalog } from \"@json-render/core\";\n36: import { schema } from \"@json-render/svelte\";\n37: import { z } from \"zod\";\n38: \n39: export const catalog = defineCatalog(schema, {\n40:   components: {\n41:     Button: {\n42:       props: z.object({\n43:         label: z.string(),\n44:         variant: z.enum([\"primary\", \"secondary\"]).nullable(),\n45:       }),\n46:       description: \"Clickable button\",\n47:     },\n48:     Card: {\n49:       props: z.object({ title: z.string() }),\n50:       description: \"Card container with title\","}
{"step_index":873,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T11:23:52Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T11:23:52Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T11:23:52Z\nShowing lines 1-50 of 64\n1: ---\n2: name: svelte-code-writer\n3: description: CLI tools for Svelte 5 documentation lookup and code analysis. MUST be used whenever creating, editing or analyzing any Svelte component (.svelte) or Svelte module (.svelte.ts/.svelte.js). If possible, this skill should be executed within the svelte-file-editor agent for optimal results.\n4: ---\n5: \n6: ## CLI tools\n7: \n8: You have access to `@sveltejs/mcp` CLI for Svelte-specific assistance. Use these commands via `npx`:\n9: \n10: ### List documentation sections\n11: \n12: ```bash\n13: npx @sveltejs/mcp list-sections\n14: ```\n15: \n16: Lists all available Svelte 5 and SvelteKit documentation sections with titles and paths.\n17: \n18: ### Get documentation\n19: \n20: ```bash\n21: npx @sveltejs/mcp get-documentation \"<section1>,<section2>,...\"\n22: ```\n23: \n24: Retrieves full documentation for specified sections. Use after `list-sections` to fetch relevant docs.\n25: \n26: **Example:**\n27: \n28: ```bash\n29: npx @sveltejs/mcp get-documentation \"$state,$derived,$effect\"\n30: ```\n31: \n32: ### Svelte autofixer\n33: \n34: ```bash\n35: npx @sveltejs/mcp svelte-autofixer \"<code_or_path>\" [options]\n36: ```\n37: \n38: Analyzes Svelte code and suggests fixes for common issues.\n39: \n40: **Options:**\n41: \n42: - `--async` - Enable async Svelte mode (default: false)\n43: - `--svelte-version` - Target version: 4 or 5 (default: 5)\n44: \n45: **Examples:**\n46: \n47: ```bash\n48: # Analyze inline code (escape $ as \\$)\n49: npx @sveltejs/mcp svelte-autofixer '<script>let count = \\$state(0);</script>'\n50: "}
